<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/advanced/time-series-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Time-series data breaks ordinary statistics' rules: the points are <strong>ordered and
    correlated with their own past</strong>. Yesterday's temperature predicts today's; this
    month's sales carry last year's echo. Analysis therefore decomposes series into
    <strong>trend + seasonality + noise</strong>, and models the dependence explicitly — which is
    why forecasting is its own craft, not regression with dates. The syllabus's toolkit —
    graphs, moving averages, trend lines, seasonal components — is exactly this decomposition
    done by hand, and doing it by hand once is the fastest way to understand what the
    forecasting software is doing for you forever after.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the classic decomposition — a rising trend, a yearly wave, and the residual noise between them" widget="plotter" config={tsCfg} />

  <p>
    Strip the seasonal wave and the straight trend from the wiggly line and what remains is the
    noise you can model. Most "surprising" business patterns dissolve into one of these three
    components — or into a comparison error (this quarter vs last, instead of this quarter vs
    <em>this quarter last year</em>).
  </p>

  <h2>Time series graphs: reading ordered data</h2>
  <p>
    The first tool is the plot itself: observations against time, joined in order. Three
    features to name before any modelling. <strong>Trend</strong> — the long-run direction
    (rising, falling, flat). <strong>Seasonality</strong> — a repeating within-year pattern of
    fixed period (ice-cream peaks every July; electricity peaks every evening as well as every
    winter — daily and annual cycles can coexist). <strong>Irregulars</strong> — the
    one-offs: a strike, a heatwave, a change in how the data is recorded. Graph-reading
    discipline matters because the eye is eager: an axis not starting at zero exaggerates
    slopes; two consecutive quarters' dips are seasonality, not collapse; and a level shift
    mid-series often marks a change in measurement (new store, new questionnaire) rather than
    in the world. Reading the graph is the exploratory stage that decides everything after:
    is the seasonal swing roughly the <em>same size</em> every year (suggesting an additive
    model) or does it grow with the level (suggesting multiplicative)?
  </p>

  <h2>Moving averages: why, and how</h2>
  <p>
    A moving average is the standard hand tool for exposing the trend, and the <em>reason</em>
    for it is precise: a simple average of one full seasonal cycle cancels the seasonal
    variation exactly (its positive and negative deviations sum to zero), so averaging over a
    window of one period leaves the trend plus smoothed noise. With quarterly data, use a
    4-point moving average; monthly, a 12-point. Worked example — quarterly sales for two
    years: 72, 86, 94, 80, then 78, 92, 100, 86.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 4-point averages: (72+86+94+80)/4 = <strong>83.0</strong>; (86+94+80+78)/4 =
    <strong>84.5</strong>; (94+80+78+92)/4 = <strong>86.0</strong>; (80+78+92+100)/4 =
    <strong>87.5</strong>; (78+92+100+86)/4 = <strong>89.0</strong>; (92+100+86+84)/4 — adding
    the first two points of year 3 (84, 98) — <strong>90.5</strong>; and so on. Notice each
    average has destroyed the seasonal shape (Q1's low, Q3's high no longer show) and the
    sequence climbs smoothly: the trend, unmasked. <strong>Plotting:</strong> with an
    <em>even</em> window there is a subtlety the exam and the software both care about — the
    first average of 72, 86, 94, 80 is centred between Q2 and Q3, not on a data point. The fix
    is <strong>centring</strong>: average consecutive moving averages in pairs, so (83.0 +
    84.5)/2 = 83.75 sits on Q3, (84.5 + 86.0)/2 = 85.25 sits on Q4, and the centred series
    83.75, 85.25, 86.75, 88.25, 89.75, 91.25 … aligns with actual quarters, rising by almost
    exactly 1.5 per quarter. (Odd windows — 3-point, 5-point — centre on data points directly
    and need no pairing, at the cost of only partly cancelling an even-period season.)
  </p>

  <h2>Trend and trend lines</h2>
  <p>
    The centred moving averages are the trend, measured. A <strong>trend line</strong>
    summarises them — drawn by eye through the centred points (older syllabuses) or fitted by
    least squares (software) — and its equation turns the description into a number: from the
    example, sales grow about 1.5 units per quarter from a base near 83.75 in year 1 quarter 3.
    Two cautions belong here. A straight trend line is a <em>model choice</em>, not a fact:
    real trends bend, saturate and reverse, and least squares will happily draw a line through
    a curve's inflection as if nothing happened. And the moving average is a
    <em>smoother</em>, not yet a forecast: it is undefined for the most recent points (the
    window looks backwards), which is why smoothing the past and predicting the future are
    different problems with different tools.
  </p>

  <h2>Seasonal variation and seasonal components</h2>
  <p>
    With the trend estimated, the seasonal pattern is what remains of the data when you subtract
    it. In the <strong>additive model</strong> — value = trend + seasonal + irregular — the
    seasonal deviation of each quarter is actual minus centred moving average. Continuing the
    example: Q1 of year 2 was 78 against a centred average of 86.75, deviation <strong>−8.75</strong>;
    Q2 was 92 against 88.25, deviation <strong>+3.75</strong>; Q3 was 100 against 89.75,
    deviation <strong>+10.25</strong>; Q4 was 86 against 91.25, deviation <strong>−5.25</strong>.
    Repeat across every year of data and <strong>average the deviations quarter by
    quarter</strong> — here the deviations repeat almost perfectly, giving seasonal components
    of Q1: −8.75, Q2: +3.75, Q3: +10.25, Q4: −5.25 (they sum to zero, as an additive set must).
    Interpretation: Q1 runs about 8.75 units below the trend, Q3 about 10.25 above. The
    <strong>deseasonalised</strong> series — actual minus seasonal component (84 − (−8.75) =
    92.75 for Q1 of year 3) — is the manager's real instrument: it removes the calendar's
    fiction so consecutive quarters can be compared honestly. In the
    <strong>multiplicative model</strong> (value = trend × seasonal × irregular), the
    components are ratios instead of differences (a Q3 component of 1.09 means "10% above the
    trend"), which is the right choice when seasonal swings scale with the level — holiday
    retail doubling, not just rising.
  </p>

  <h2>Predictions from trend lines — and their limitations</h2>
  <p>
    Forecasting with this toolkit is arithmetic: extend the trend line forward, add the
    appropriate seasonal component. From the example, the trend value four quarters on is
    roughly 83.75 + 1.5 × 7 ≈ 94.3, and the Q3 seasonal component adds +10.25, so a Q3
    forecast ≈ <strong>104.5</strong>. Now the limitations, which are the real lesson.
    Extrapolation assumes the trend <em>continues</em> — markets saturate, competitors arrive,
    pandemics interrupt; a straight line says nothing about the break that ends it, and the
    further ahead you forecast, the more of the prediction is assumption rather than data.
    The trend line carries no error estimate: a single number masquerades as a certain one
    where honest practice reports an interval. Structural breaks (a new product line, a
    re-based index) invalidate the whole decomposition, and the irregular component is by
    definition unforecastable — a good forecast can still be wrong by its own irreducible
    noise. The professional habit: forecast with intervals, backtest against held-out data,
    and state what would falsify the trend assumption.
  </p>
  <h2>Seasonal adjustment in the wild — and index numbers</h2>
  <p>
    The mechanics above run inside every headline statistic you have ever read. Retail-sales
    reports, unemployment series and GDP are published <em>seasonally adjusted</em> — the
    statistical office estimates and removes the calendar pattern (the same additive or
    multiplicative decomposition, industrialised in software such as X-13-ARIMA-SEATS) so that
    a December-to-January retail crash reads as "down 0.3% adjusted" rather than "down 20%,
    because Christmas ended". Two refinements matter at this scale. First, the seasonal
    component itself drifts — holiday timing shifts, behaviour changes — so it is re-estimated
    continually, and <em>why</em> last quarter's figure was revised is often "the seasonal
    factors were revised", not the economy changing. Second, series of money get the
    <strong>index-number</strong> treatment: a price series is deflated into a base year
    (CPI rebased to 100 in some reference year) so "the trend" is measured in constant
    purchasing power — comparing nominal sales across a decade of inflation measures the
    currency, not the business. A time series, in other words, is never raw: by the time it
    reaches a chart it has been seasonalised, deflated, revised and re-based, and the
    sophisticated reader asks which of those operations happened before trusting the trend
    the chart appears to show.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "removing seasonality means the seasonal pattern doesn't
    matter." Deseasonalising is for comparison, not for dismissal — the summer surge is real
    revenue and real staffing need. The point is to stop attributing the calendar to the
    strategy: after deseasonalising, a flat quarter reads as flat, not as "down 10% from
    summer, disaster".
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why ordered data needs its own statistics.</strong> The correlation that ordinary
    regression assumes away — each point with its own past — is real and measurable: the
    <strong>autocorrelation function</strong> (correlation of the series with itself lagged 1,
    2, 3, … periods) is a time series' fingerprint. Seasonality appears as spikes at the
    seasonal lags; a slow decay marks a trend; and the presence of autocorrelation at all is
    why ordinary regression p-values on time series are unreliable — the effective sample size
    is smaller than the point count suggests. <strong>Stationarity</strong> — statistical
    properties constant over time — is the standing assumption most business series violate,
    and <strong>differencing</strong> (modelling the changes rather than the levels) is the
    classical remedy: the "I" in ARIMA. <strong>The model zoo:</strong> the random walk
    (tomorrow = today + noise) models efficient-market prices, for which past direction carries
    no exploitable information; <strong>ARIMA</strong> combines autoregressive terms (a
    weighted memory of past values), differencing and moving-average terms (a memory of past
    shocks) and has powered demand planning for fifty years; <strong>exponential
    smoothing</strong> weights recent observations more heavily, and its Holt–Winters
    extension — level, trend and seasonal components, each with its own smoothing constant —
    still beats fancier methods on many business series. <strong>Spurious regression</strong>
    is the field's standing warning: two independent trending series (UK GDP and your
    conference travel budget) will show impressive correlation and tiny p-values when regressed
    on each other in levels; the cure is detrending/differencing, the vaccine is scepticism.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> how to forecast well under regime change (the rare-event
    problem all history is a small sample), hierarchical reconciliation (forecasts that add up
    across product and region), and the evaluation of very-long-horizon forecasts, where the
    data to verify them outlives the careers that made them.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Box and Jenkins, Time Series Analysis (1970); Hyndman and Athanasopoulos, Forecasting: Principles and Practice (free online); Yule (1926) on spurious correlation.</p>
</TopicPage>
